<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luxor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Luxor Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luxor is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, built on the site of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. Its great temple complexes, including Luxor Temple and nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>, were developed during the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. They reflect the wealth and religious importance of Thebes and the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> in this period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luxor.jpg" alt="Luxor" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Unger · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LuxorHotelsIbnWalidSt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Luxor lies about 670 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and about 220 km north of Aswan. The ruins of the temple complexes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and Luxor stand within the modern city, and across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> lie the temples and tombs of the West Bank Theban Necropolis, which includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a> and the Valley of the Queens. The name comes from the Arabic al-quṣūr, the palaces, perhaps equivalent to the Greek and Coptic names meaning three castles. Its ancient Egyptian name was Waset, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> or Diospolis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> began to grow into a thriving city in the 11th Dynasty, under Montuhotep II, who united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>. It played a major part in expelling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, and from the 18th to the 20th Dynasty it was the political, religious and military capital of Egypt. Expeditions to Kush and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> brought it great wealth, and it drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoans</a>. A Hittite prince even came to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a>'s widow, Ankhesenamun. Its principal god was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, worshipped with his wife Mut and their son Khonsu, and linked with the sun god Ra as Amun-Ra.</p><p>In the Late Period the political centre moved to northern cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bubastis">Bubastis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> stayed the religious capital until the Greek period, and the temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> was the most important in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> until the end of antiquity. The Assyrian emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> attacked the city, which fell into ruins and lost significance. Christian monks later set up monasteries among the monuments, including at the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>, now Deir el-Bahri. After the Muslim conquest part of Luxor Temple was converted from a church into the mosque now called the Abu Haggag Mosque. Europeans visited in growing numbers in the 18th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Luxor is the capital of the Luxor Governorate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> and is among the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world. It has often been called an open-air museum, and it is a major destination for tourists. Its monuments, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>, Luxor Temple and the Theban Necropolis, together with the temples, churches and mosques of the city, make up a layered cultural heritage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luxor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The temple complexes, most prominently Karnak and <b>Luxor</b>, were at once religious centres, economic institutions, and state treasuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Since then the army has faced Islamist insurgency: Al-Gama'a al-Islamiyya's attacks on officials, foreigners and Copts, the <b>Luxor</b> massacre of 62 in 1997, and the killing of at least 305 at a Sufi mosque in Sinai in November 2017.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They met their gods at festivals, when the statue was carried out in a portable boat, as in the Opet festival at Thebes, when Amun travelled from Karnak to <b>Luxor</b>; they also consulted the gods as oracles, whose movements answered questions.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In the village of Zainiya, north of <b>Luxor</b>, passive speakers over 50 years old were recorded as late as the 1930s, and traces of the old spoken language were reported at Abydos and Dendera.</blockquote><a href="/language/coptic">Coptic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Papyrus of Ani, regarded as the finest surviving example, was found in <b>Luxor</b> in 1888 by Egyptians trading in illegal antiquities and acquired by E. A. Wallis Budge for the British Museum, where it remains.</blockquote><a href="/work/book-of-the-dead">Book of the Dead · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Merneptah of the Nineteenth Dynasty commemorated his victories over the Sea Peoples on the walls of the Cachette Court, the start of the processional route, also called the Avenue of Sphinxes, to <b>Luxor Temple</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/karnak">Karnak · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
